WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.920
If you’ve ever thought to yourself that
your family’s Christmas traditions were

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.330
a bit, well… weird, then you’re not alone!

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.629
There are some incredibly strange legends
and customs surrounding Christmas time, and

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:17.060
what you do on Christmas day can be vastly
different, depending on where you live.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:25.570
Here are seven of the strangest Christmas
traditions from around the world.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:31.480
Iceland has its fair share of strange Christmas
legends, from child-eating cats to Grýla

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:38.120
the troll, but one in particular, the Yule
Lads, holds a place in every Icelandic child’s

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.120
heart.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.950
In the past, they were seen as the mischievous
sons of mountain trolls, harassing farmers

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.440
and the general population.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.480
But over time, the Yule Lads became much more
benevolent, in the same way Santa Claus is

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.289
portrayed today, as a magical, stocking-filling,
bringer of joy.

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:58.429
On the night before December 12th children
all over Iceland would put one of their shoes

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:04.159
out on the windowsill, then head to bed with
a smile on their faces…or a worried frown

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:05.909
if they’d misbehaved.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:11.140
The thirteen Yule Lads would then come down
one by one on the nights leading up to Christmas

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.310
Eve.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.030
Depending on whether the children had been
good or bad, the Yule Lads would leave sweets

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.610
and presents, or rotten potatoes in their
shoes.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.540
While potato-ey shoes were definitely a real
concern for some kids, the Icelandic Folk

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.950
Customs branch had to step in and discourage
parents, from giving out such extravagant

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:37.280
gifts, as the Yule Lads were seen to be favouring
some kids far more than others.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.810
The awareness campaign advised parents, through
media and kindergartens, hoping to reduce

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.750
the festive gap between rich and poor families.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.150
The festive spirit of the Yule Lads remains
well and strong today, perhaps because, today,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.470
they more closely resemble Santa Clause, than
the more sullen image of morose mountain trolls

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:00.229
that their ancestors from the 1700s possessed.

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:05.649
Every year in the Cuban town of Remedios,
a transformation takes place.

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:11.000
In the run up to Christmas, the town splits
in two, and the preparation for the Parrandas

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.290
festival begins.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:19.180
There is a longstanding tradition of competition
between two neighbourhoods in the town; San

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:24.490
Salvador, represented by a rooster, and El
Carmen, represented by a Hawk.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:29.420
These two sides of the town prepare all year
to battle it out for the title of… best

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.410
Christmas festival.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.190
This isn’t your usual friendly competition
between you and your neighbour to see who

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:41.410
has the most Christmas lights, this is an
all-out battle extravaganza that goes back

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.570
centuries.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:47.050
Not only do the residents meticulously decorate
their houses in the themes and colours of

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:52.620
their district, but each hosts their own fireworks
show and float to be presented in the main

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.810
display.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:58.860
Magnificent costumes and dancing in the streets
to a wide orchestra of music, accompanies

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.340
the festivities.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:04.550
You would think you’re at “Carnival,”
except there are Christmas decorations everywhere

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.430
instead of Samba dancers.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:11.260
The festival finds its roots in the 18th century
when the priest in the Grand Cathedral of

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.150
Remedios sent out children to gather people
for Christmas mass.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:20.260
The children would have pots, pans and spoons,
and would run around the town making noise

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.690
and singing songs to attract people and lead
them to the mass.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:31.080
Over time this developed into the fantastically
extravagant street party that we see today.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:37.670
While this isn’t necessarily a “strange”
tradition, it’s one of unusual intensity,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.750
even when compared to hotspots of Christmas
festivities around Britain and the United

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:42.750
states.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:49.370
The festivities are so extravagant and infectious
that nearby Cuban towns host their own versions

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.560
with their own competing districts every year.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.520
I’m sure you’ve had Christmas carollers
knocking on your door and singing songs to

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.480
you before… but what about Mummers?

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.420
This bizarre tradition actually has a few
forms across most of Europe, but in Latvia,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.360
it is particularly well-established and accepted
as a local custom.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:16.200
Latvian “mumming,” done by “mummers”
is a bit like the traditional Christmas carollers,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.250
but there’s a twist.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:23.840
Mummers dress up as all kinds of creatures
from wolves, to goats, fortune tellers, and

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.730
bears, and other amazingly surreal costumes.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:31.230
They are usually warmly welcomed into homes
as they are believed to drive away evil spirits

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.560
with their costumes, dancing, and singing.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.070
These days, it’s more of a festive tradition
for good food and company than a superstitious

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.070
one.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:44.220
If you happen to recognize the voices of any
of the mummers, then they must immediately

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.480
take off their mask.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.200
Mummers usually alter their voices and mannerisms
to avoid being recognized when mumming, sometimes

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.770
adopting peculiar and eccentric personas.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:05:00.660
In the end, there’s a lot of joyful singing,
eating and dancing, which adds to the local

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.620
festive spirit, which is always nice around
Christmas.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:08.930
Latvia isn’t the only country where a Christmas
tradition has developed that results in food

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.350
and drink for the performers.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:17.550
Something similar, but with a wackier twist,
takes place across villages in southern Wales.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.690
Mari Lwyd is a local folk custom that takes
place on Christmas.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:26.190
The actual meaning behind “Mari Lwyd”
is disputed to this day.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:31.250
Some people think it means “Grey Mare,”
referring to the horse head, as well as “lwyd”

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.940
being a common term for grey.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.870
Others believe it translates to “Holy Mary,”
a key figure in Welsh Catholicism.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.170
Whatever it means, it’s definitely recognizable
when it comes to your village.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:46.790
Groups of men and women, often dressed in
flamboyant costumes such as Punch and Judy,

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:52.060
would carry the Mari Lwyd, which is a horse’s
skull on top of a stick, dressed in a frock,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.660
through towns - whilst playing music.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.450
They would travel from house to house and
when they got to your door, they would break

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.300
out in song, asking to be invited inside.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.780
It’s customary to sing back and if you choose
to, deny entry with an excuse.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.340
This would go back and forth until either
the troop gives up, leaving and going to the

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:17.300
next house, or the homeowner runs out of excuses,
and is obliged to let them in.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:22.230
Once inside, the troop puts on a performance,
with the Mari Lwyd chasing around adults and

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:27.780
children alike, snapping its jaws with the
leader pretending helplessly to subdue it.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.530
Mari Lwyd isn’t the only troublemaker in
the troop either, as Punch will even use his

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:37.750
poker to rake out the fire if the homeowner
didn’t forbid him, before they entered the

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:38.790
house.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:43.160
Food and drinks would be served to the troop
as thanks for their performance and in return

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.650
for their blessing.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:49.910
The earliest records of this bizarre tradition
come from the 19th century, but the actual

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:51.840
origins aren’t clear.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.450
Many think the Mari Lwyd is an offshoot from
pre-Christian, pagan rituals, involving other

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.450
hooded animals.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:03.850
Either way, it’s definitely meant as a goodwill
sentiment, but at the same time, it’s certainly

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:06.889
one of the stranger Christmas traditions out
there.

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:10.040
Nothing says merry Christmas like a horse’s
skull.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:15.940
Christmas in Venezuela is a particularly important
time of the year in the heavily catholic country.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:21.110
But in the capital of Caracas, they take it
to a whole new and…exciting level for the

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.930
pious morning journey to mass.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:29.050
Something which has seemingly developed out
of nowhere, is the tradition of roller-skating

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.680
to church during the weeks leading up to Christmas.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.340
From the 16th to 24th December, most roads
are closed until 8am to allow the skaters

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.530
to get to mass safely.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.210
During this time, you will see streets full
of people skating by with their families.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.540
Because the children usually go to bed early
for the morning mass, they have devised a

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.370
cunning way to get up on time.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:57.470
Some children tie string around their big
toe and dangle it from their bedroom window.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:01.260
Roller-skaters passing by will tug on the
string to wake the children up.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:05.610
Personally, I prefer using an alarm clock
to get up in the morning, but I guess getting

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.419
pulled out of bed by your big toe is also
viable option, if you’re a particularly

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:09.419
heavy sleeper!

00:08:09.419 --> 00:08:13.740
Remember that time you beat a log with a stick
while singing a song, to make it poop out

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.720
a shiny new bicycle?

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:16.720
No?

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.680
Well that’s what children all over Catalonia
will be doing this Christmas.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.060
It sounds bizarre, but it’s actually an
extremely common tradition that has a big

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.460
following in the Catalonian community.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:33.130
It’s centred on decorating and looking after
Tio de Nadal, “The Christmas Log.”

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:37.969
The log is actually pretty adorable when fully
decorated, with a painted face, a hat and

00:08:37.969 --> 00:08:41.930
a blanket, with some versions having little
legs that prop it up.

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:45.430
Leading up to Christmas, it’s customary
to give “Tio” a little bit of food to

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.690
eat and cover him with a blanket to keep him
comfortable and warm.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.450
The better looked-after Tio is, the better
the presents he is supposed to provide, I

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:56.450
say “provide” but I actually mean crap
out.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:01.080
Each family has a slightly different version
of the tradition, but generally, on Christmas

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:07.670
day they put Tio halfway into the fire and
beat it with sticks to make it “defecate”

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:08.670
presents.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.210
No, really.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:17.290
There are actually very popular songs that
are also sung during the log-beating, gift-defecation

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:18.570
ceremony.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:22.620
Parents usually send the kids out and ask
them to pray for good gifts while they beat

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.260
the log, as a nifty way to sneak presents
under Tio’s blanket whilst they’re not

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:28.260
looking.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:32.370
Even though the way Tio is supposed to produce
the presents is a little…odd, it’s still

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.230
easy to see the charm of the little wooden
fellow.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:39.760
The lyrics to the songs are also pretty interesting
to say the least.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:45.080
You probably decorate your Christmas tree
with lights, tinsel and baubles, but in Ukraine

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:49.050
you might find something pretty unexpected
draped around the tree.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:54.350
It’s common for people there to decorate
their trees with fake webs and even ornamental

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:55.350
spiders.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:59.960
It might seem like they are a few months late
to Halloween, but there’s actually a reason

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.200
for the creepy-crawly shenanigans.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:07.660
The story goes that a poor, but hardworking
widow lived in a small hut with her children.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.560
One fateful day, a pine cone fell to the earthen
floor and took root, invigorating the children

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.270
with the chance of having a Christmas tree
by winter.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:20.130
The children took care of the tree, helping
it grow, but by the time Christmas came around,

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:24.320
the poor family didn’t have the money to
decorate it, so they went to bed on Christmas

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.690
Eve with heavy hearts.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.680
When they woke up on Christmas day, they were
surprised to find the tree was covered in

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:36.300
spider’s webs, and as they opened the windows
to get a better look, the sunlight hit the

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.650
webs, turning them to silver and gold.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:43.370
Needless to say, the family never lived in
poverty again, or at least… that’s how

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:44.660
the folk-tale goes.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:50.160
This legend is widely believed as the origin
of putting tinsel on Christmas trees, representing

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.510
the gold and silver of the transformed spider’s
webs.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:58.190
It’s amazing to see just how many different
ways people celebrate Christmas, all over

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:59.420
the world.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:05.320
Some may be stranger than others, but all
of them are based on the same, universal Christmas

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:06.320
spirit.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.640
Thanks for the view and merry Christmas.

